{"step_index":88,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-30T13:58:21Z","input_tokens":2832,"cache_read_tokens":113080,"output_tokens":4849,"content":"# QUEUE ORACLE 🏥\n### AI-Powered Smart Hospital Queue & Resource Prediction System\n\n> **Vision:** *\"Queue Oracle does not just show what is happening in a hospital. It predicts what could happen next and helps hospitals prepare.\"*\n\nA complete, fully functional end-to-end prototype of **Queue Oracle** has been built and tested in your project workspace at:\n`C:\\Users\\KRUTIKA SHIVANAND\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\queue-oracle`\n\n---\n\n## 1. Project Directory Structure\n\n```\nqueue-oracle/\n│\n├── app.py                     # Main Flask REST API & Web Router\n├── database.py                # SQLite schema (8 relational tables) & connection helpers\n├── data_generator.py          # Synthetic dataset generator (>1500 records) & DB seeder\n├── train_model.py             # ML training (Random Forest & Linear), evaluation & saving\n├── prediction.py              # ML inference & dynamic queue delay recalculation engine\n├── disease_forecasting.py     # Aggregate epidemiological analysis & tomorrow's forecast\n├── resource_management.py     # Bed occupancy (+1h-4h forecast), inventory & staffing\n├── recommendations.py         # Doctor workload rebalancing & admin decision support\n├── forecasting.py             # Diurnal hourly arrivals & waiting time curve modeling\n├── test_app.py                # 10/10 automated end-to-end integration test suite\n├── requirements.txt           # Python dependencies\n├── README.md                  # Detailed documentation & hackathon jury guide\n│\n├── dataset/\n│   ├── synthetic_patients.csv # 1,500 realistic historical patient training records\n│   └── historical_disease.csv # 30-day surveillance history across 5 conditions\n│\n├── models/\n│   ├── wait_time_rf.joblib     # Saved Random Forest Regression model artifact\n│   ├── wait_time_linear.joblib # Saved Multiple Linear Regression baseline artifact\n│   └── model_metrics.json      # Actual validation metrics (MAE, RMSE, R², \n<truncated 7724 bytes>\nmulation verified\n[TEST 09 PASS] Inventory risk analysis and restock verified\n[TEST 10 PASS] ML Metrics verified: Random Forest R² = 0.9962, MAE = 10.0 mins\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. How to Present This at the Hackathon (3rd-Semester Guide)\n\n1. **The Hook (1 minute):**\n   > *\"Existing hospital OPD systems only tell a patient who is currently inside the clinic. They don't predict how long you will wait, whether a doctor is overloaded, or if the hospital will run out of beds in three hours. Queue Oracle transforms hospital management from reactive to predictive.\"*\n\n2. **The Dynamic Queue Demo (2 minutes):**\n   - Go to **Patient Queue** (`Section 3`). Point out **Dr. Rajesh Sharma** with an active patient.\n   - Click **Record Delay** and enter `25` minutes. Show the judges how all subsequent patient consultation clocks automatically shift forward by 25 minutes.\n   - Go to **Wait Time Prediction** (`Section 4`), type token `101`, and show the explainable input weights.\n\n3. **The Intelligent Rebalancing Demo (1 minute):**\n   - Go to **Reassignment Recommendations** (`Section 10`).\n   - Explain: *\"Dr. Rajesh Sharma has 16 patients waiting, while Dr. Priya Nair has only 3. The system calculates that transferring 6 patients saves 55 minutes of queue time.\"*\n   - Click **APPROVE REASSIGNMENT** and show the judges that both queues rebalance instantly.\n\n4. **Resource & Disease Forecasting (1.5 minutes):**\n   - Go to **Bed Occupancy** (`Section 7`): Show the **+1h, +2h, +3h, +4h** forecast chart and the Cardiology bed shortage warning.\n   - Go to **Disease Trends** (`Section 8`): Show the 14-day Dengue surge and explain how it automatically flags low stock in **Dengue NS1 Antigen Kits** under **Inventory** (`Section 9`).\n\n5. **ML Performance (`Section 13`):**\n   - Show the live comparison table: *\"We trained a Random Forest model with 1,500 historical records. It achieves an $R^2$ of 0.996 and an MAE of 10 minutes, significantly outperforming linear regression because triage priority and doctor fatigue are non-linear relationships.\"*","thinking":"Completed the assigned task. The PowerShell command initiates a new process, which may introduce a binding delay, especially in sandboxed environments. Unit tests, executed successfully, cover all routes using a test client.\n\n\nAll core functionalities and project requirements are verified as implemented and returning expected results. Comprehensive testing confirms the system addresses OPD tokens, queue imbalances, bed shortages, and disease surge scenarios. The technology stack aligns with the project specifications, and the administrator dashboard provides the required data points.\n\n\nThe system now predicts patient waiting times, incorporating factors like doctor availability, patient priority, and department load. Dynamic recalculation addresses consultation delays, updating expected times for all queued patients under the same doctor, and incorporating early completions. Patient consultation status tracking provides a workflow for moving through WAITING, CALLED, IN_CONSULTATION, and COMPLETED stages, updating the active queue and recording actual consultation duration when complete.\n\n\nThe system now integrates doctor availability updates, recalculating patient wait times and refreshing department workload data on the dashboard. Developed a disease trend analysis module predicting future cases of key illnesses by analyzing trends. Implemented intelligent doctor workload balancing, recommending patient reassignments to address imbalances and estimate wait time reductions, subject to approval. The system now predicts department-wise bed occupancy, forecasting potential shortages and suggesting administrative actions. Inventory and medicine preparedness is connected to the disease trend analysis.\n\n\nDeveloped an IV fluid alert system; also predicting staffing needs using department demand and patient-to-doctor ratios, pinpointing strained areas. An administrator decision support system now gives recommendations with impact analysis and prioritized actions, including approval workflows and histories.\n\nAn interactive simulation lab allows evaluating diverse scenarios by manipulating patient arrivals, priority, and resource changes. Integrated data visualizations present key metrics using nine charts.\n\n\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
