{"step_index":100,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-30T14:08:42Z","input_tokens":2375,"cache_read_tokens":123009,"output_tokens":2864,"content":"Here is your **complete, step-by-step testing guide**. Follow these exact steps in your browser to verify that every layer—the backend, the database, the machine learning model, and the user interface—is genuinely working together in real time.\n\n---\n\n### Prerequisites: Open the Dashboard\n1. Open your web browser (Chrome, Edge, or Firefox).\n2. Go to: **[http://127.0.0.1:5000](http://127.0.0.1:5000)**\n3. You will see the **Queue Oracle** hospital dashboard with a dark navy sidebar on the left and live KPI cards at the top.\n\n---\n\n### TEST 1: Verify the AI Model & Token Prediction (Section 4)\n*Goal: Prove that wait times are calculated by a real Machine Learning model and not hardcoded.*\n\n1. In the left sidebar, click **`4. Wait Time Prediction`**.\n2. In the input box, type **`101`** and click the blue **Predict Waiting Time** button.\n3. **What to look for on screen:**\n   - A detailed card appears displaying:\n     - **OPD Token:** `#101`\n     - **Assigned Doctor:** `Dr. Rajesh Sharma (Room 101)`\n     - **Predicted Waiting Time:** Calculated in minutes\n     - **Expected Consultation Time:** An exact clock time (e.g., `10:45 AM`)\n     - **AI Explainability Box:** Shows the model inputs (Priority code, doctor speed, queue ahead).\n4. Now test a different token: type **`105`** and search again.\n   - Notice that the wait time, expected consultation clock, and patients ahead change dynamically because Token #105 is deeper in the queue.\n\n---\n\n### TEST 2: Verify Dynamic Delay Cascading (Section 3 & 5)\n*Goal: Prove that when one consultation is delayed, all downstream patients' wait times automatically push forward.*\n\n1. In the sidebar, click **`3. Patient Queue`**.\n2. Scroll to the top clinic card: **Dr. Rajesh Sharma (General Medicine)**.\n3. Look at the green box showing who is currently inside:\n   - Notice the patient in consultation (e.g. Token #101).\n   - Now look at the table right below it at **Patient #1 in line** (e.g. Token #102) and note their current predicted wait time (e.g. **`10 min`**) and expected time (e.\n<truncated 5167 bytes>\nent Queue & Doctor Load Simulator**:\n   - Select any doctor from the dropdown.\n   - Click **`+10 Normal Patients`**: Watch the doctor's queue instantly surge.\n   - Click **`Toggle Doctor Break`**: The doctor is marked \"ON BREAK\" and patients' wait times adjust.\n   - Click **`Complete Current Consultation`**: The active patient is checked out, and the next patient is called.\n3. **Reset to Clean State:**\n   - When you are done playing with the simulation, click the red button: **`Reset Simulation to Baseline`**.\n   - The database instantly re-seeds itself back to the clean starting state!\n\n---\n\n### TEST 8: Machine Learning Performance Verification (Section 13)\n*Goal: Show the evaluator your actual machine learning accuracy and explainability.*\n\n1. Click **`13. ML Performance`** in the sidebar.\n2. Point out these genuine, calculated metrics to the evaluator:\n   - **Random Forest:** $R^2 \\approx 0.996$ (explains 99.6% of wait time variance), $\\text{MAE} \\approx 10\\text{ minutes}$.\n   - **Linear Regression Baseline:** $R^2 \\approx 0.860$, $\\text{MAE} \\approx 59.7\\text{ minutes}$.\n   - **Feature Importance Chart:** Explains that `patients_ahead` (42%) and `avg_consult_duration` (28%) have the greatest impact on waiting times.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Checklist to Show the Evaluator:\n| What to Show | How to Show It |\n|---|---|\n| **AI Wait Time Prediction** | Go to Section 4 $\\rightarrow$ Enter token `101` $\\rightarrow$ Show predicted clock & explainable inputs. |\n| **Dynamic Delay Recalculation** | Go to Section 3 $\\rightarrow$ Click `Record Delay` (+25m) $\\rightarrow$ Show all subsequent tokens shift forward. |\n| **Intelligent Rebalancing** | Go to Section 10 $\\rightarrow$ Click `Approve Reassignment` $\\rightarrow$ Show 6 patients move from Dr. Sharma to Dr. Nair. |\n| **Bed Shortage Forecasting** | Go to Section 7 $\\rightarrow$ Show Cardiology ward saturated at +3 hours. |\n| **Disease $\\rightarrow$ Inventory Link** | Go to Section 8 $\\rightarrow$ Simulate Dengue spike $\\rightarrow$ Show Section 9 trigger kit shortage alarm. |","thinking":"The goal is to create a step-by-step guide for a hackathon participant to verify end-to-end functionality. The guide needs to be actionable and cover all components: backend, database, machine learning model, and UI.\n\nFocusing on the user's need for a concrete guide, the initial approach will be to break down the verification into distinct stages, starting with the simplest, most observable element and working toward more complex interactions. The guide will include actions the user should perform along with expected results.\n\n\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
